Run this machine's two-model development loop on the task below. You are the
**orchestrator**: you dispatch work, you don't do the reasoning or the coding
yourself.

**Task:** $ARGUMENTS

Await the Planner's terminal output before starting Builder; never pre-spawn
Builder. Use one Planner and one Builder in this pass. At most one
plan-authorized L1/FE delegation is allowed; multi-workstream work requires an
explicit Team Leader call.

Steps:

1. **Plan ({{PLANNER_MODEL}}).** Delegate to the `planner` subagent. Give it the
   task and any relevant context you already have. It runs on the configured
   planner model class and returns a completed reviewed plan: verified current
   state and done-condition; the smallest real end-to-end slice; non-goals or
   deferred work; a simpler rejected alternative; exact surfaces and step →
   verification actions; earliest behavioral/live proof; and stop/replan plus
   install/rollback risks. Do not start writing code yourself.

2. **Review.** Read the plan. If it's sound, briefly summarize it for the user
   and continue. If it has a real gap or a decision only the user can make, raise
   that before building — otherwise reject or revise the plan before proceeding.

3. **Build ({{BUILDER_MODEL}}).** Delegate to the `builder` subagent, handing it
   the planner's plan verbatim plus any decisions from step 2. It runs on the
   configured builder model class, implements the plan end to end, runs the
   relevant build/tests, and reports what changed and how it verified it. On the
   first failed or inconclusive real proof, it stops scope expansion and only
   diagnoses or retries the same slice.

4. **Light audit (enabled={{WORKFLOW_AUDIT_ENABLED}}; {{WORKFLOW_AUDIT_MODEL}}, {{WORKFLOW_AUDIT_THINKING}} thinking).**
   If enabled, delegate once to `workflow-audit` with the original task, planner
   output, executor identity (`builder` or the specialist key), and executor
   result. Skip this step when disabled or when the executor failed. This is a
   concise read-only review, not the full direct-call Audit agent. It must not
   edit, delegate, or restart the workflow.

5. **Report.** Relay what was built, how it was verified, the light-audit verdict,
   and anything left open. If the build or audit surfaced something that
   invalidates the plan, report the follow-up clearly rather than hiding it.

Honor the local project `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md` conventions throughout.

---

*To keep going until a done-condition holds:* use
`/pbg <task> until: <condition>`. That is the **portable** path — every harness
this registry installs into renders the same bounded plan → build → verify loop.
`/pb is exactly one pass` and reports incomplete evidence rather than retrying;
`/pbg is capped at exactly three rounds`. A second inconclusive proof or two
rounds without measurable progress is BLOCKED even if artifact labels change.
Persistence language cannot override ambiguity, safety, failed proof,
no-progress, or round bounds.

<!-- Claude Code only — not part of the shared PB contract. -->
*Claude Code additionally has a harness-enforced loop:* run the built-in
`/goal <condition>` first, then `/pb <task>` — its Stop-hook keeps working until
the condition holds or you run `/goal clear`. It is stronger than `/pbg` because
the harness enforces it rather than the model, but it exists nowhere else.
`/goal` and `/pb` can't share one message, so send them as two.
